  "severity": "Important",
  "description": "Package updates are available for Amazon Linux 2023 that fix the following vulnerabilities:\nCVE-2026-99288:\n\tIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ninet: reject unhashed sockets during established lookups\n\nTCP established ehash lookups are lockless. The socket caches involved use SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU, so memory can be reused before an RCU reader that retained its old address has finished.\n\nAfter a tuple match, __inet_lookup_established() and __inet6_lookup_established() acquire a reference and repeat the tuple check. They do not check whether the new object is hashed. If the address is reused for a socket with the same tuple, the lookup can acquire a reference to the new object and return it while its nulls node is unhashed.\n\nA PoC that drives reuse with a SYN cookie request socket reproduced the crash. The new request socket is unhashed and has a NULL rsk_listener. A lookup that had loaded the address of the old request socket acquired the reference published by tcp_get_cookie_sock() and returned the new object. tcp_v4_rcv() then read sk_state through the NULL listener.\n\nThe resulting crash was:\n\n  BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 0000000000000012\n  #PF: supervisor read access in kernel mode\n  #PF: error_code(0x0000) - not-present page\n  ...\n  Oops: Oops: 0000 [#1] SMP NOPTI\n  CPU: 4 UID: 0 PID: 0 Comm: swapper/4 Not tainted 7.3.0-00591-g0e135910deec #1 PREEMPT(lazy)\n  ...\n  RIP: 0010:tcp_v4_rcv+0x44e/0x1280\n  ...\n  R10: 0000000000000001 R11: 00000000024ca8c0 R12: 0000000000000000\n  ...\n  CR2: 0000000000000012 CR3: 0000000103d50004 CR4: 0000000000770ef0\n  ...\n  Call Trace:\n   \u003cIRQ\u003e\n   ? raw_local_deliver+0xae/0x240\n   ip_protocol_deliver_rcu+0x2d/0x180\n   ip_local_deliver_finish+0x84/0x100\n   ip_sublist_rcv_finish+0x5f/0x70\n   ip_sublist_rcv+0x12a/0x1d0\n   ip_list_rcv+0xfd/0x130\n   __netif_receive_skb_list_core+0x223/0x250\n   netif_receive_skb_list_internal+0x1d2/0x2c0\n   ? try_fill_recv+0x39/0x7b0\n   napi_complete_done+0x74/0x170\n   virtnet_poll+0x445/0x600\n   __napi_poll+0x27/0x1e0\n   net_rx_action+0x2fe/0x3b0\n   handle_softirqs+0xc2/0x2c0\n   __irq_exit_rcu+0x74/0xd0\n   common_interrupt+0x7f/0x90\n   \u003c/IRQ\u003e\n  ...\n  Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception in interrupt\n\nAfter taking a reference, check whether the nulls node is unhashed in both IPv4 and IPv6 established lookups. If it is unhashed or the tuple no longer matches, drop the reference and restart the lookup.\n\nLookups racing with the TIME_WAIT hashdance, or with a request being replaced by its child, now restart and find the new socket instead of returning the old one.\n\nRequest sockets that were never inserted into ehash can have a nonzero refcount, and a stale lookup can hold a transient reference. Therefore, when SYN cookie, Fast Open, and request migration paths fail after setting a nonzero refcount, release the references held by those paths with reqsk_put() instead of freeing the request directly.\n",
